[{"text":"Pair[x, y] is the head of a special pairing used in the internal representation: x and y may have heads LorentzIndex or Momentum.","anchor":"pair"},{"text":"If both x and y have head LorentzIndex, the metric tensor (e.g. g^{\\mu \\nu}) is understood.","anchor":"pair"},{"text":"If x and y have head Momentum, a scalar product (e.g. \\(p \\cdot q\\)) is meant.","anchor":"pair"},{"text":"If one of x and y has head LorentzIndex and the other Momentum, a Lorentz vector (e.g. \\(p^{\\mu }\\)) is implied.","anchor":"pair"},{"text":"Overview, FV, FVD, MT, MTD, ScalarProduct, SP, SPD.","anchor":"see-also","section":"See also"},{"text":"This represents a \\(4\\)-dimensional metric tensor","anchor":"examples","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Alpha]], LorentzIndex[\\[Beta]]]","anchor":"cb1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{g}^{\\alpha \\beta }\\]","anchor":"cb1-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"This is a D-dimensional metric tensor","anchor":"cb1-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Alpha], D], LorentzIndex[\\[Beta], D]]","anchor":"cb2","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[g^{\\alpha \\beta }\\]","anchor":"cb2-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"If the Lorentz indices live in different dimensions, this gets resolved according to the t’Hooft-Veltman-Breitenlohner-Maison prescription","anchor":"cb2-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Alpha], n - 4], LorentzIndex[\\[Beta]]]","anchor":"cb3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[0\\]","anchor":"cb3-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"A \\(4\\)-dimensional Lorentz vector","anchor":"cb3-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Alpha]], Momentum[p]]","anchor":"cb4","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^{\\alpha }\\]","anchor":"cb4-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"A \\(D\\)-dimensional Lorentz vector","anchor":"cb4-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Alpha], D], Momentum[p, D]]","anchor":"cb5","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[p^{\\alpha }\\]","anchor":"cb5-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\(4\\)-dimensional scalar products of Lorentz vectors","anchor":"cb5-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[Momentum[q], Momentum[p]]","anchor":"cb6","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}\\cdot \\overline{q}\\]","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[Momentum[p], Momentum[p]]","anchor":"cb7","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^2\\]","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[Momentum[p - q], Momentum[p]]","anchor":"cb8","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}\\cdot (\\overline{p}-\\overline{q})\\]","anchor":"cb8-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[Momentum[p], Momentum[p]]^2","anchor":"cb9","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^4\\]","anchor":"cb9-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Pair[Momentum[p], Momentum[p]]^3","anchor":"cb10","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^6\\]","anchor":"cb10-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"ExpandScalarProduct[Pair[Momentum[p - q], Momentum[p]]]","anchor":"cb11","section":"Examples"}]